From: Sebastian Matkovich <sebastian.matkovich@gmail.com>
Date: Fri, 30 Oct 2026 20:20:00 +0100
Subject: [PATCH] misc: fastrpc: freeze a user's invocation in place instead
 of interrupting it

A user's FASTRPC_IOCTL_INVOKE waits for the DSP interruptibly. Every
system suspend interrupts it: the freezer's fake signal ends the wait with
-ERESTARTSYS, which leaves the context queued with the DSP and moves the
user's maps to the channel's invoke_interrupted_mmaps, and after the thaw
the restarted ioctl sends the same call again as a new context. The DSP
then has the call twice.

hexagonrpcd, the reverse-RPC daemon for the DSPs' file system, spends
nearly all its time in such a call (its listener waits there for the
DSP's next request). On the F(x)tec Pro1X (SM6115) the first s2idle
ended exactly so: 2 ms after "Restarting tasks" the ADSP's sensor process
died in its FastRPC thread

  PDM: service 'sensor_process' crash: 'EX:sensor_process:0x1:frpc_dsp:0xc0:PC=0xb2022ad0'
  qcom_q6v5_pas a400000.remoteproc: fatal error received: err_qdi.c:1045:EX:sensor_process:...

remoteproc restarted the ADSP, and the APR audio services timed out
(-110) until a reboot. postmarketOS and Debian work around it by stopping
hexagonrpcd for every suspend. Downstream's adsprpc restores the
interrupted context on the restart instead of sending the call again.

Wait with TASK_KILLABLE | TASK_FREEZABLE, as qcomtee's user objects and
FUSE do: the freezer freezes the task where it waits, nothing is
interrupted or sent twice, and a DSP answer that arrives meanwhile is
taken at the thaw. Fatal signals still end the wait (so an unhandled
SIGTERM still stops hexagonrpcd); other signals wait for the call to
return. No locks are held across the wait.

freezable_invoke=N (module parameter, writable at runtime) brings back
the interruptible wait.
---
diff --git a/drivers/misc/fastrpc.c b/drivers/misc/fastrpc.c
index f3a4938458..91b0397876 100644
--- a/drivers/misc/fastrpc.c
+++ b/drivers/misc/fastrpc.c
@@ -17,6 +17,7 @@
 #include <linux/sort.h>
 #include <linux/of_platform.h>
 #include <linux/rpmsg.h>
+#include <linux/sched.h>
 #include <linux/scatterlist.h>
 #include <linux/slab.h>
 #include <linux/firmware/qcom/qcom_scm.h>
@@ -1238,6 +1239,25 @@ static int fastrpc_invoke_send(struct fastrpc_session_ctx *sctx,
 
 }
 
+/*
+ * A user's invocation waits for the DSP's answer killable and freezable:
+ * for a system suspend the task is frozen in place, waiting, instead of
+ * being interrupted. Interrupted (-ERESTARTSYS), the context stays queued
+ * with the DSP, the user's maps move to invoke_interrupted_mmaps, and the
+ * restarted ioctl sends the same call again as a new context. For a
+ * daemon such as hexagonrpcd, whose reverse-RPC listener is blocked in an
+ * invocation nearly all the time, every suspend did that: the DSP got its
+ * listener call twice and its process died at the thaw (on the F(x)tec
+ * Pro1X "EX:sensor_process:0x1:frpc_dsp:0xc0", the ADSP restarted, its
+ * audio services gone until a reboot). Fatal signals still end the wait;
+ * other signals are taken once the call has returned.
+ * freezable_invoke=N: the old interruptible wait.
+ */
+static bool freezable_invoke = true;
+module_param(freezable_invoke, bool, 0644);
+MODULE_PARM_DESC(freezable_invoke,
+		 "User invocations frozen in place for a suspend, not interrupted (default Y)");
+
 static int fastrpc_internal_invoke(struct fastrpc_user *fl,  u32 kernel,
 				   u32 handle, u32 sc,
 				   struct fastrpc_invoke_args *args)
@@ -1276,6 +1296,9 @@ static int fastrpc_internal_invoke(struct fastrpc_user *fl,  u32 kernel,
 	if (kernel) {
 		if (!wait_for_completion_timeout(&ctx->work, 10 * HZ))
 			err = -ETIMEDOUT;
+	} else if (READ_ONCE(freezable_invoke)) {
+		err = wait_for_completion_state(&ctx->work,
+						TASK_KILLABLE | TASK_FREEZABLE);
 	} else {
 		err = wait_for_completion_interruptible(&ctx->work);
 	}
